  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Damping and Dissipation",
    "lesson_core_move": "Relate damping and dissipation mechanisms in continuous-time physical systems to decaying responses that support BIBO stability by preventing bounded inputs from accumulating into growing or unbounded outputs.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Damping and Dissipation",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Damping and Dissipation",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Damping and dissipation are the mechanisms that make a continuous-time response lose energy as time passes. In stability work, the cue to look for is a response that shrinks rather than accumulates, because decay is what keeps bounded input data from turning into growing output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The simplest model is an exponentially decaying response. When the response has the form $y(t)=K e^{-a t}u(t)$ with $a>0$, the damping rate is positive and the response is forced toward zero instead of away from it.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=K e^{-a t}u(t),\\ a>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That sign matters. A positive decay rate makes the factor $e^{-a t}$ shrink over time, so the response settles instead of accumulating. This is the basic damping signature used throughout the topic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{t\\to\\infty} K e^{-a t}=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The contrast case is the same exponential shape with the opposite sign in the exponent. If the exponent is positive, the response does not decay, so the output can grow instead of settling.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=K e^{+a t}\\text{ does not decay for }a>0\\text{ and can grow unbounded.}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the first rule for this topic is simple: positive damping produces decay, decay produces settling, and settling is the behavior that prevents bounded inputs from building into unbounded outputs. Later examples will reuse this sign test as the quickest way to recognize a stable response mechanism.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Damping and Dissipation",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying a Decaying Response from Damping",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify $y(t)=3e^{-1.8t}$ as decaying or non-decaying and compute its long-term limit.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the exponent sign. A response of the form $K e^{-a t}$ with $a>0$ is the standard damped form from the tutorial, so this example is already set up to decay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Rewrite the response in that standard form by reading off the coefficient and decay rate:",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=3e^{-1.8t}=K e^{-a t},\\qquad K=3,\\ a=1.8>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $a>0$, the exponential factor shrinks as $t$ increases. That means the response is decaying, not growing, so the output settles instead of accumulating.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{t\\to\\infty} 3e^{-1.8t}=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the correct classification is decaying, and the long-term limit is $0$. That is the exact behavior damping is meant to produce: a bounded response that settles rather than building into an unbounded output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Damping and Dissipation",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Relating Dissipation to Output Settling",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $y_1(t)=4e^{-0.7t}$ and $y_2(t)=4e^{-2.7t}$, identify which has stronger dissipation and quicker settling.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the decay rate in the exponent. Both responses are already in the damped form $K e^{-a t}$, so the comparison reduces to reading off $a$ and deciding which response loses energy faster.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Extract the decay rates from each response and keep the amplitudes fixed so only the dissipation strength changes:",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "$y_1(t)=4e^{-0.7t}$ so $a_1=0.7$, and $y_2(t)=4e^{-2.7t}$ so $a_2=2.7$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $2.7>0.7$, the second response has the stronger dissipation. A larger positive decay rate makes the exponential factor shrink faster, so $y_2(t)$ settles sooner and stays less persistent than $y_1(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Both responses still decay to zero, but the one with the larger $a$ reaches that settled behavior more quickly. That is the practical meaning of stronger dissipation: it suppresses long-lived output and reduces the chance that bounded input data can accumulate into a growing response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Damping and Dissipation",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Checking a Bounded Input Against a Damped Response",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $|x(t)|\\le 3$ and $y(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-2(t-\\tau)}x(\\tau)d\\tau$, bound $|y(t)|$ and conclude if the output is bounded.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a bounded input passed through a damped response kernel. The exponential factor $e^{-2(t-\\tau)}$ decays in time, so the right move is to bound the output by the input envelope and then evaluate the resulting integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|y(t)|\\le\\int_0^t e^{-2(t-\\tau)}|x(\\tau)|d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the given bound $|x(\\tau)|\\le 3$ and keep the damping kernel fixed. That gives a purely numerical envelope for the output magnitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|y(t)|\\le 3\\int_0^t e^{-2(t-\\tau)}d\\tau=\\frac{3}{2}(1-e^{-2t})<\\frac{3}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is finite for every $t$ and stays below $\\frac{3}{2}$, so the output is bounded. This is the BIBO pattern in its simplest damped form: a bounded input does not accumulate without limit because the decaying kernel suppresses long-term growth.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Damping and Dissipation",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing How Damping Prevents Accumulation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compare $y_d(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-(t-\\tau)}x(\\tau)d\\tau$ and $y_u(t)=\\int_0^t x(\\tau)d\\tau$ for bounded input $x(t)=1$, and identify which accumulates.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that both systems see the same bounded input, so the only difference is whether the response kernel damps old input or keeps adding it directly. That makes this a direct test of how damping prevents accumulation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the bounded input $x(\\tau)=1$ into each response and keep the notation fixed:",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_d(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau,\\qquad y_u(t)=\\int_0^t 1\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now evaluate each expression. The damped kernel produces a settled response, while the undamped integrator keeps accumulating the input over time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_d(t)=1-e^{-t},\\qquad y_u(t)=t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The damped response stays bounded and approaches $1$, but the undamped response grows without bound. So the one that accumulates is $y_u(t)$, and the one that avoids accumulation is $y_d(t)$; this is exactly how damping blocks bounded input from becoming unbounded output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "question_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Damping and Dissipation",
    "lesson_core_move": "Relate damping and dissipation mechanisms in continuous-time physical systems to decaying responses that support BIBO stability by preventing bounded inputs from accumulating into growing or unbounded outputs.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Damping and Dissipation",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying a Decaying Response from Damping",
        "step_type": "example",
        "question_type": "multiple-choice",
        "answer_cardinality": "single",
